      "reasoning": "All 16 requested answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. The source error is confined to q-34403's explanation: it incorrectly says 7\\pi/6 lies in quadrant IV; that angle lies in quadrant III. The intended coterminal angle is 7\\pi/4, and the final answer IV is correct.\nq-73485: The cofactor transpose divided by 12 gives the displayed inverse.\nq-18288: The inverse entry is C12/71; C12=-((-1)(3)-2(5))=13.\nq-18286: The inverse entry is C23/39; C23=-(1(-1)-4(0))=1.\nq-73535: C33=3, so 3/(6+3k)=1/4 gives k=2.\nq-7608: The ellipse has horizontal semiaxis 3 and vertical semiaxis 5, giving x²/9+y²/25=1.\nq-34403: Adding 4\\pi to -9\\pi/4 gives 7\\pi/4, in quadrant IV.\nq-122043: Canceling factorials gives 3/5.\nq-124405: 5!-0!=120-1=119.\nq-13536: A+B-C-B+C=A.\nq-13564: Row products give -12+16=4 and 16-20=-4.\nq-70190: Row products give -3+8=5, -3+24-4=17, and -6-10=-16.\nq-13556: Row products give 3b+4b=7b and 3c+4c=7c.\nq-19220: The projection coefficient is (36/6)cos(135°)=-3\\sqrt{2}.\nq-126507: a·b=21 and a·a=63, so the projection is a/3=⟨2,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩.\nq-28902: cos(x)=1/2 gives \\pi/3 and 5\\pi/3; their product is 5\\pi²/9.\nq-29182: cos(x)=\\sqrt{3}/2 gives -330° and -30° in the specified interval; their numerical product is 9900. No submitted grade or earlier answer was supplied; each MA answer is established by its captured worked solution.",
